/* Trick shorts for Bio + QR - the 15-second fixes the mini-test review routes to.
   Merged into window.TOPICS so the Trick Series player renders them via ?topic=<key>,
   without listing them on the 14-Trick hub. Authored + fact-verified (bio-evolution author-only, HW facts checked). */
window.TOPICS = Object.assign(window.TOPICS||{}, {"bio-biomolecules":{"tag":"Bio - one move","title":"Biomolecules: Four Bricks, Four Builds","hookTag":"Stop the memory dump","hookWord":"You don't have four biomolecules to memorize.<br/>You have <em>four LEGO sets.</em>","hookSub":"Each one snaps together from one kind of brick. Learn the brick, and the whole macromolecule falls out of it.","formula":"<span class=\"teal\">One brick</span> &rarr; one build: monosaccharide &rarr; polysaccharide, amino acid &rarr; protein, nucleotide &rarr; nucleic acid, fatty acid + glycerol &rarr; fat. <span class=\"gold\">The brick names the build, and the build names the job.</span> <span class=\"warm\">Lipids ride along, but they aren't a true polymer.</span>","formulaSub":"All four snap together by dehydration synthesis (lose a water); all four come apart by hydrolysis (add a water). Same reaction, run four ways.","bulletsHead":"The 4 moves","bullets":["<em>Read the brick.</em> Sugar ring = carb. Amino group with an R-group = protein. Sugar-phosphate-base = nucleic acid. Long greasy tail = lipid.","<em>Name the build + the bond.</em> Monosaccharide &rarr; polysaccharide (glycosidic), amino acid &rarr; protein (peptide), nucleotide &rarr; nucleic acid (phosphodiester), fatty acid + glycerol &rarr; fat (ester).","<em>Same reaction, four times.</em> Build by dehydration synthesis (lose a water), break by hydrolysis (add a water).","<em>Let structure call function.</em> R-groups fold a protein into its shape, hydrophobic tails wall off a membrane, the ordered bases along the sugar-phosphate backbone store the code."],"caption":"Four monomers, four macromolecules. Match the brick to the build and Bio biomolecules stop being a memory dump. #DAT #DATprep #predental #biology #premed #studytok","trapH":"Lipids aren't a true polymer","trapSub":"The \"four polymers\" line is a shortcut. Carbs, proteins, and nucleic acids are real polymers of a repeating monomer. Lipids are just glycerol plus fatty acids linked by ester bonds, so never call a fat a polymer of fatty acids. One more high-yield miss: the nucleic acid monomer is the whole nucleotide (sugar + phosphate + base), not the base alone.","lockWord":"Brick names the build; build names the job.","lockDecode":"<span class=\"teal\">Spot the monomer</span> and you know the macromolecule it builds, and once you know the macromolecule you know what it does &rarr; sugar for <span class=\"gold\">energy</span>, protein for work, nucleotide for information, lipid for membranes and storage.","speak":{"hook":"Stop treating biomolecules like four random lists to cram. Think of them as four LEGO sets. Each macromolecule snaps together from one kind of brick, so if you learn the brick, the whole thing falls right out of it.","formula":"One brick, one build. A monosaccharide gives you a polysaccharide, an amino acid gives you a protein, a nucleotide gives you a nucleic acid, and a fatty acid plus glycerol gives you a fat. The brick names the build, and the build names the job. Just remember lipids ride along here, but they are not a true polymer.","trap":"Here is where people lose the point. The four polymers line is a shortcut. Carbs, proteins, and nucleic acids are real polymers built from a repeating monomer, but a lipid is just glycerol plus fatty acids on ester bonds, so never call a fat a polymer of fatty acids. And the nucleic acid monomer is the whole nucleotide, sugar plus phosphate plus base, not the base by itself.","lock":"Lock it in with this: brick names the build, build names the job. See the monomer, name the macromolecule, and you instantly know its function. Sugar for energy, protein for work, nucleotide for information, lipid for membranes and storage."}},"bio-organelles":{"tag":"Bio - one move","title":"Every Organelle Is One Job","hookTag":"Bio - cell structure","hookWord":"Stop memorizing the <em>diagram</em>.<br/>Start reading the <em>job</em>.","hookSub":"An organelle isn't a shape on a map. It's a worker hired to do exactly one task &mdash; name the task, not the picture.","formula":"One organelle &rarr; <span class=\"gold\">one job.</span> Don't picture the map &mdash; <span class=\"teal\">read the job title.</span>","formulaSub":"Mitochondrion = ATP. Ribosome = protein. Nucleus = DNA. Chloroplast = sugar.","bulletsHead":"The four jobs","bullets":["<em>Mitochondrion &rarr; ATP.</em> The power plant burns fuel to make usable energy.","<em>Ribosome &rarr; protein.</em> The assembly line reads mRNA and builds the chain.","<em>Nucleus &rarr; DNA.</em> The vault stores the blueprint and hands out copies.","<em>Chloroplast &rarr; sugar.</em> Plant-only: sunlight in, glucose out."],"caption":"Every organelle is one job. Read the job, name the part. #DAT #biology #premed #studytok","trapH":"Don't reverse the sugar","trapSub":"Chloroplasts <em>make</em> sugar from sunlight; mitochondria <em>burn</em> sugar to make ATP. Plants have both &mdash; animals have mitochondria only.","lockWord":"One organelle, one job.","lockDecode":"See an organelle name? Skip the diagram and ask <span class=\"gold\">what's its one job?</span> The job <span class=\"teal\">is</span> the answer.","speak":{"hook":"Stop trying to memorize the whole cell diagram. Every organelle is just a worker with exactly one job. Learn the job, and the name comes for free.","formula":"Here is the move: one organelle, one job. Mitochondria make ATP. Ribosomes make protein. The nucleus holds DNA. Chloroplasts make sugar. Four names, four jobs, done.","trap":"The trap is reversing the sugar. Chloroplasts make sugar using sunlight, and mitochondria burn that sugar to make ATP. Plants have both -- you have mitochondria only.","lock":"So when a question names an organelle, do not picture the map. Ask one thing: what is its job? One organelle, one job -- read the job and you have the answer."}},"bio-transport":{"tag":"Bio - one move","title":"Membrane transport: which way is the hill running?","hookTag":"The reframe","hookWord":"It's not the molecule.<br/>It's the <em>hill</em>.","hookSub":"Every membrane transport question is secretly one question: which way is the gradient running?","formula":"Down the gradient &rarr; <span class=\"teal\">free — passive / diffusion</span><br/>Up the gradient &rarr; <span class=\"warm\">costs ATP — active</span><br/><span class=\"gold\">Osmosis = water moves toward more solute</span>","formulaSub":"Down is free. Up costs ATP. And water follows the solute, not the reverse.","bulletsHead":"The 4 reads","bullets":["<em>Find the hill.</em> One side crowded, one side empty? Moving toward the empty side is downhill.","<em>Downhill = passive.</em> No ATP. Covers simple diffusion, facilitated diffusion, and osmosis.","<em>Uphill = active.</em> Burns ATP. The sodium-potassium pump shoves 3 Na out, 2 K in, against both gradients.","<em>For water, watch the solute.</em> Osmosis pulls water toward the side with more solute."],"caption":"The membrane transport read nobody spells out for you: stop memorizing types, just find the hill. Down is free, up costs ATP, water chases the solute. #DAT #premed #biology #MCAT","trapH":"A protein does not mean ATP","trapSub":"You see a channel or carrier and assume the cell is spending energy. It isn't. A protein is just a door. If the molecule is still riding its gradient downhill, that's facilitated diffusion — passive, zero ATP. Energy is only spent when you push uphill.","lockWord":"Downhill free, uphill you pay, water chases the salt.","lockDecode":"<span class=\"teal\">Downhill</span> = passive, no ATP. <span class=\"warm\">Uphill</span> = active, burns ATP. <span class=\"gold\">Osmosis</span> = water moves toward the side with more solute.","speak":{"hook":"Stop memorizing transport types. Picture a hill instead. If the molecule is rolling from a crowded side to an empty side, that is downhill, and downhill is free. If you are shoving it from empty toward crowded, that is uphill, and uphill always costs ATP.","formula":"So the whole thing collapses to one question: which way is the gradient running? High to low is passive -> simple diffusion, facilitated diffusion, and osmosis, all free. Low to high is active -> the cell burns ATP, like the sodium-potassium pump pushing three sodium out and two potassium in against both gradients. For water, do not track the water, track the solute: osmosis moves water toward the side with more solute.","trap":"Here is the trap that gets people. You see a membrane protein, a channel or a carrier, and you assume it must cost energy. It does not. A channel is just a door. If the molecule is still moving down its gradient, that is facilitated diffusion, and it is passive, zero ATP. A protein being involved never makes it active by itself.","lock":"Lock it in: downhill free, uphill you pay, water chases the salt. Down the gradient is passive, up the gradient burns ATP, and osmosis always sends water toward the saltier side."}},"bio-respiration":{"tag":"Bio - one move","title":"Cellular Respiration: Oxygen Is the Drain, Not the Fuel","hookTag":"Bio - the reframe","hookWord":"Oxygen isn't the fuel.<br/>It's the <em>drain</em>.","hookSub":"Cells \"burn glucose with oxygen,\" sure, but oxygen never actually touches the sugar. It waits at the very end of the line to catch the leftover electrons. That one shift unlocks the whole pathway.","formula":"Glucose &rarr; strip its electrons onto <span class=\"gold\">NADH / FADH2 &rarr; electron transport chain</span> &rarr; <span class=\"teal\">O2 catches them last &rarr; becomes water &rarr; 30+ ATP</span><br/><span class=\"warm\">No O2 &rarr; chain stalls &rarr; fermentation &rarr; net 2 ATP</span>","formulaSub":"Same glucose every time. The only variable: is O2 there to catch the electrons at the end?","bulletsHead":"Follow the electrons","bullets":["<em>Glycolysis</em>, in the cytoplasm: split glucose into 2 pyruvate. Net 2 ATP, plus 2 NADH loaded. No oxygen needed yet.","<em>Krebs cycle</em>, in the matrix: strip the remaining carbons off as CO2 and charge up the carriers, NADH and FADH2.","<em>Electron transport chain</em>, on the inner membrane: carriers drop the electrons down, protons get pumped, ATP synthase spins out the big haul.","<em>Oxygen at the very end</em>: it grabs the spent electrons, joins with protons, and becomes water. That is the drain that keeps the whole line moving."],"caption":"Oxygen isn't the fuel, it's the drain. Pull it and every cell crashes from thirty-plus ATP down to a measly net 2. That gap is the whole difference between breathing and fermenting. #DAT #premed #biology #cellularrespiration #studytok","trapH":"\"Fermentation makes the 2 ATP\"","trapSub":"It doesn't. Glycolysis makes those 2 ATP. Fermentation makes zero, its only job is to regenerate NAD+ so glycolysis can keep turning without oxygen. Same error in reverse: don't say O2 builds ATP either. O2 only catches the final electrons.","lockWord":"No O2, no chain, net 2.","lockDecode":"Take <span class=\"warm\">O2</span> away and the transport <span class=\"gold\">chain</span> has nothing to accept the electrons, so it stalls. You fall back to glycolysis alone &rarr; <span class=\"warm\">net 2 ATP</span>. Keep <span class=\"teal\">O2</span> as the final acceptor and the full chain runs &rarr; <span class=\"teal\">30+ ATP</span>.","speak":{"hook":"Everybody says cells burn glucose with oxygen. But here is the twist: oxygen never even touches the sugar. It sits at the very end of the line, waiting to catch the electrons after glucose has already been taken apart.","formula":"Follow the electrons. Glucose gets torn apart and its high-energy electrons load onto carriers, NADH and FADH2. Those carriers drop the electrons down the transport chain, protons get pumped, and ATP synthase spins out the big haul, pushing the total past thirty ATP. Then oxygen catches the spent electrons at the very end and becomes water.","trap":"Here is the trap. When I say no oxygen gives you net two ATP, do not credit fermentation for those two. Fermentation makes zero ATP by itself. Those two come from glycolysis, and fermentation's only job is to recycle NAD+ so glycolysis can keep running.","lock":"Lock it in: no O2, no chain, net two. Take oxygen away and the transport chain has nothing to accept the electrons, so it stalls. You are left with glycolysis alone, and glycolysis nets you two ATP. That is why oxygen is the drain, not the fuel."}},"bio-photosynthesis":{"tag":"Bio - one move","title":"Photosynthesis: where the oxygen really comes from","hookTag":"The reframe","hookWord":"The O2 a plant releases came from <em>water</em> &mdash;<br/>not the CO2 it breathes in.","hookSub":"Two reactions. Two rooms. Two totally different jobs.","formula":"<span class=\"teal\">Light reactions</span> (thylakoid): split <span class=\"gold\">water</span> &rarr; O2 released, plus ATP + NADPH.<br/><span class=\"warm\">Calvin cycle</span> (stroma): spend that ATP + NADPH to fix <span class=\"gold\">CO2</span> &rarr; sugar.","formulaSub":"Water in, oxygen out. CO2 in, sugar out.","bulletsHead":"Trace it in 4 beats","bullets":["Light hits the thylakoid and <em>splits water</em> — that is where the O2 comes from.","The light reactions bank the energy as <em>ATP</em> and <em>NADPH</em>.","The Calvin cycle in the stroma grabs <em>CO2</em> and spends that ATP + NADPH to build sugar.","So the halves are linked: light reactions <em>pay</em>, Calvin cycle <em>spends</em>."],"caption":"Water in, oxygen out. CO2 in, sugar out. Learn the two rooms and photosynthesis stops being a blur.","trapH":"The oxygen is NOT from CO2","trapSub":"Everyone assumes the O2 a plant releases comes from the carbon dioxide it takes in. It does not. Every oxygen atom in that gas was ripped off a <em>water</em> molecule during the light reactions. The carbon from CO2 gets built into <em>sugar</em> instead — it never leaves as the gas.","lockWord":"Water for air, carbon for sugar.","lockDecode":"<span class=\"gold\">Water</span> gets split to make the O2 in the <em>air</em> (light reactions). The <span class=\"gold\">carbon</span> in CO2 gets fixed into <em>sugar</em> (Calvin cycle). Two inputs, two outputs, two rooms.","speak":{"hook":"Quick one that trips up almost everyone. When a plant pumps out oxygen, where do those oxygen atoms actually come from? Not the carbon dioxide it breathes in. They come straight from water.","formula":"Split photosynthesis into two jobs. Job one, the light reactions, happen in the thylakoid: light splits water, oxygen leaves as gas, and you bank the energy as ATP and NADPH. Job two, the Calvin cycle, happens in the stroma: it spends that ATP and NADPH to pull carbon dioxide in and build sugar.","trap":"Here is the trap. On test day you will want to say the oxygen came from carbon dioxide. It did not. Every oxygen atom released came off a water molecule. The carbon from CO2 gets built into sugar, not released as the gas.","lock":"Lock it in like this. Water for air, carbon for sugar. Water gets split to make the oxygen in the air. Carbon from CO2 gets built into sugar. Two rooms, two jobs, done."}},"bio-central-dogma":{"tag":"Bio - one move","title":"Central Dogma: transcribe vs translate","hookTag":"Bio - central dogma","hookWord":"<em>Transcribe</em> vs <em>translate</em>:<br/>one word tells them apart.","hookSub":"They sound like twins, so students swap them. But the names literally spell out the difference.","formula":"DNA &rarr; <span class=\"gold\">transcription</span> &rarr; RNA &rarr; <span class=\"gold\">translation</span> &rarr; protein<br/>Transcribe = <span class=\"teal\">same language</span> (nucleotides). Translate = <span class=\"warm\">new language</span> (amino acids).","formulaSub":"Transcribe keeps you in nucleotide alphabet (DNA to RNA); translate jumps to the amino-acid alphabet (RNA to protein).","bulletsHead":"The 4 beats","bullets":["<em>Transcription:</em> read the DNA template strand and lay down mRNA as its complement (template A gives U, T gives A, G gives C, C gives G).","<em>Swap the letter:</em> RNA has no thymine, so every T becomes U, and U pairs with A.","<em>Ship it:</em> the finished mRNA leaves the nucleus and the ribosome reads it in 3-base codons.","<em>Translation:</em> each codon specifies one amino acid, and the growing chain folds into a protein (a stop codon signals the end)."],"caption":"Transcription copies DNA into RNA (T becomes U, and the mRNA is the template's complement). Translation reads that RNA into protein. Same language, then a new one.","trapH":"Copying the template as-is","trapSub":"The mRNA is the template's complement, not a duplicate, so a template A gives you U, not A. And if a thymine ever shows up in your RNA, it is wrong: RNA uses uracil.","lockWord":"Transcribe = same language. Translate = new language.","lockDecode":"Same language means DNA to RNA: still nucleotides, you just swap <span class=\"warm\">T</span> for <span class=\"teal\">U</span> and build the complement of the template. New language means RNA to protein: the ribosome reads <span class=\"gold\">three letters at a time</span> and turns them into amino acids.","speak":{"hook":"Transcription and translation sound like twins, and that is exactly why they trip you up. Here is the fix: transcribe means rewrite in the same language, translate means switch to a new one. DNA and RNA are both nucleotide language, so moving between them is transcription. Protein is a different language, amino acids, so getting there is translation.","formula":"Start at DNA and move one arrow at a time. DNA -> RNA is transcription, RNA -> protein is translation. When you build the RNA, you read the template strand and lay down its complement, and every time you would write a T you write a U instead. So the mRNA is not a photocopy of the template, it is its complement, with uracil in place of thymine.","trap":"The number one trap is copying the template strand letter for letter. The mRNA is the complement of the template, not a duplicate, so a template A gives you a U and a template G gives you a C. And never write thymine in RNA. If you see a T sitting in your messenger RNA, you made a mistake, because RNA uses uracil.","lock":"Lock it with this: transcribe is same language, translate is new language. Same language means DNA to RNA, still nucleotides, you just swap T for U and take the complement. New language means RNA to protein, where the ribosome reads three letters at a time and builds amino acids into a protein."}},"bio-cell-division":{"tag":"Bio - one move","title":"Mitosis vs Meiosis: count the splits","hookTag":"Cell division - the reframe","hookWord":"Stop counting phase names.<br/><em>Count the divisions.</em>","hookSub":"One number - how many times the cell splits - decides identical vs shuffled, and diploid vs haploid.","formula":"Copy your DNA once. Then ask one question: how many times do you divide?<br/><span class=\"teal\">Mitosis: divide 1x &rarr; 2 identical diploid clones</span><br/><span class=\"warm\">Meiosis: divide 2x &rarr; 4 shuffled haploid cells</span><br/><span class=\"gold\">One copy, two splits = chromosome number cut in half</span>","formulaSub":"Both copy the DNA exactly once. The split count is the entire difference.","bulletsHead":"Run it in 4 steps","bullets":["<em>Same start:</em> S phase copies every chromosome once, so each is now two sister chromatids joined at the centromere.","<em>Mitosis</em> - one division pulls the sisters apart &rarr; 2 cells, still <em>diploid (2n)</em> and genetically identical.","<em>Meiosis I</em> - splits the homologous <em>pairs</em> (not the sisters). This is the halving step: 2n &rarr; n.","<em>Meiosis II</em> - splits the sisters &rarr; 4 <em>haploid</em> cells, each non-identical from crossing over and independent assortment."],"caption":"The whole question is: how many times did it divide? One split = 2 identical twins. Two splits = 4 shuffled cells at half the chromosomes. Save this before your next Bio set.","trapH":"The halving is in Meiosis I, not II","trapSub":"Everyone assumes meiosis II cuts the number - it does not. The chromosome number halves in meiosis I, when the homologous pairs separate. Meiosis II only pulls sister chromatids apart (a mini mitosis) and keeps the count the same.","lockWord":"Copy once, count the splits.","lockDecode":"You copy the DNA a single time either way. <span class=\"teal\">One split &rarr; 2 identical diploid cells (mitosis)</span>. <span class=\"warm\">Two splits &rarr; 4 non-identical haploid cells (meiosis)</span>. Dividing twice off that one copy is <span class=\"gold\">why the chromosome number halves.</span>","speak":{"hook":"Stop memorizing all the phase names. The only thing the DAT is really testing here is how many times the cell divides. One division or two -> that single number tells you everything else about the daughter cells.","formula":"Both pathways start identical: S phase copies every chromosome one time. Mitosis then divides once, so you get two cells that are still diploid and genetically identical. Meiosis divides twice off that same single copy, so you get four cells, each haploid and non-identical. Copy once, divide twice, and the chromosome number gets cut in half.","trap":"Here is where people lose the point. They think meiosis two does the halving. It does not. The number gets cut in meiosis one, when the homologous pairs separate. Meiosis two just pulls sister chromatids apart, exactly like a mini mitosis, and the count stays the same.","lock":"So count the splits. One split gives you twins, two identical diploid cells. Two splits gives you a shuffle, four non-identical haploid cells. Same single DNA copy either way, which is exactly why dividing twice halves the chromosomes."}},"bio-mendel":{"tag":"Bio - one move","title":"Mendelian Genetics: Draw the Box, Read the Ratio","hookTag":"Bio - one move","hookWord":"Recessive isn't <em>weak.</em><br/>It's <em>hiding.</em>","hookSub":"One dominant allele covers it up completely. The recessive trait only shows its face when it has nothing dominant to hide behind.","formula":"Cross <span class=\"teal\">Bb</span> x <span class=\"warm\">Bb</span> in a 2x2 box. <span class=\"teal\">B (dominant)</span> masks <span class=\"warm\">b (recessive)</span>. Boxes fill in: BB, Bb, Bb, bb &rarr; <span class=\"gold\">3 show dominant : 1 shows recessive.</span> Only bb &mdash; two lowercase &mdash; shows the recessive trait.","formulaSub":"The four boxes <em>are</em> the ratio. You count them, you don't memorize them.","bulletsHead":"Draw it in 4","bullets":["Write each parent's two alleles &mdash; one down the side, one across the top (Bb and Bb).","Fill each of the four boxes by pulling one letter from its row and one from its column.","Read the trait: any box with a capital shows <em>dominant</em>; only the all-lowercase box (bb) shows <em>recessive</em>.","Count the boxes for the phenotype ratio &rarr; Bb x Bb gives <em>3 dominant : 1 recessive</em>, every time."],"caption":"Draw the box. Big letter wins. The ratio was sitting there the whole time. #DAT #biology #genetics #premed #mendelian","trapH":"Bb looks identical to BB","trapSub":"A carrier (Bb) is invisible &mdash; it looks exactly like a pure dominant (BB). You can't read genotype off looks unless the trait is recessive (bb). So the 3:1 you <em>see</em> is the phenotype, but the genotype underneath is 1:2:1 &mdash; 1 BB : 2 Bb : 1 bb. Only 1 of the 3 dominant boxes is truly BB.","lockWord":"Big letter wins, small needs a twin.","lockDecode":"One capital allele always <span class=\"teal\">masks</span> the trait. The <span class=\"warm\">recessive</span> version only appears when two lowercase alleles pair up &rarr; <span class=\"gold\">bb</span>. That's the whole rule.","speak":{"hook":"Here is the reframe. A recessive trait is not weak, it is hiding. One dominant allele is enough to cover it up completely. It only shows its face when there is nothing dominant standing in front of it.","formula":"Set up the Punnett square. Cross Bb by Bb, one parent down the side, one across the top. Fill the four boxes: capital B, the dominant, masks lowercase b, the recessive. You get BB, Bb, Bb, bb, which is three showing the dominant trait to one showing recessive. The ratio falls right out of the box.","trap":"Do not fall for this. A carrier, big B little b, looks exactly like a pure dominant, big B big B. You cannot tell them apart just by looking. Only the double recessive, little b little b, actually shows the recessive trait. So the three to one ratio you see is the phenotype, but the genotype underneath is one to two to one, one pure dominant, two carriers, one recessive. Only one of those three dominant boxes is truly pure dominant.","lock":"Lock it in. Big letter wins, small needs a twin. One capital masks the trait every single time, and the recessive only shows up when two lowercase alleles pair together as little b little b."}},"bio-evolution":{"tag":"Bio - one move","title":"Aim, Wander, Double the Middle","hookTag":"Bio - Evolution &amp; Pop Gen","hookWord":"Two forces change a gene pool.<br/>One <em>aims</em>. One <em>wanders</em>.","hookSub":"And Hardy-Weinberg is just the ruler that says <em>nothing changed at all</em>. Learn to tell them apart and this whole unit collapses into one line.","formula":"<span class=\"teal\">Selection</span> = non-random &rarr; <em>fitness</em> picks the winners<br/><span class=\"warm\">Drift</span> = random &rarr; <em>chance</em> picks the winners<br/><span class=\"gold\">Hardy-Weinberg:</span> p + q = 1 &rarr; p&sup2; + 2pq + q&sup2; = 1","formulaSub":"Heterozygotes (the carriers) = <span class=\"gold\">2pq</span> — that middle term <em>always</em> wears a 2.","bulletsHead":"Solve any HW problem in 4 beats","bullets":["<em>Start recessive.</em> The recessive phenotype is the only group you can count straight up &rarr; it equals q&sup2;. Take the square root to get q.","<em>Get p.</em> p = 1 &minus; q. Do <em>not</em> read p off the dominant phenotype — carriers are hiding in there.","<em>Read the genotypes.</em> Homozygous dominant = p&sup2;, carriers = <span class=\"gold\">2pq</span>, homozygous recessive = q&sup2;.","<em>Name the force.</em> Changed by fitness &rarr; <span class=\"teal\">selection</span>. Changed by luck in a small population &rarr; <span class=\"warm\">drift</span>."],"caption":"Selection aims. Drift wanders. Carriers are double. That's the entire unit in one breath. #DAT #DATprep #biology #hardyweinberg #premed","trapH":"Dropping the 2 in 2pq","trapSub":"Carriers are <em>2pq</em>, never pq. And you can't read p off the dominant phenotype, because p&sup2; <em>and</em> 2pq both look dominant — the carriers are camouflaged in there. Always start from the recessives (q&sup2;), take the square root, then p = 1 &minus; q.","lockWord":"Aim, wander, double the middle.","lockDecode":"<span class=\"teal\">Aim</span> = selection, non-random and fitness-directed. <span class=\"warm\">Wander</span> = drift, random chance (loudest in small populations). <span class=\"gold\">Double the middle</span> = heterozygotes are 2pq — start from q&sup2;, get p = 1 &minus; q, and the carriers are always double.","speak":{"hook":"Every evolution question is really one gene pool changing over time. There are only two ways it changes: one aims, and one wanders. Selection aims because fitness picks the winners. Drift wanders because pure chance picks the winners.","formula":"Write it in a single line. Selection is non-random and fitness-based. Drift is random. Then Hardy-Weinberg is your no-evolution ruler: p plus q equals 1, and the genotypes are p squared plus 2 p q plus q squared equals 1. The carriers, the heterozygotes, are 2 p q -- and that middle term always wears a 2.","trap":"Here is where people lose the points. They try to read p straight off the dominant phenotype, but p squared and 2 p q both look dominant, so the carriers are hiding inside that number. Start from the recessives instead: that group is q squared, take the square root to get q, then p equals 1 minus q. And never write carriers as p q -- it is 2 p q.","lock":"Lock it in three moves: aim, wander, double the middle. Aim is selection, wander is drift, and double the middle means the heterozygotes are 2 p q. Start recessive, get p, double the middle -- that one habit solves the math and names the force."}},"bio-physiology":{"tag":"Bio - one move","title":"Follow the Blood","hookTag":"Physiology - one sentence","hookWord":"The heart isn't one pump.<br/>It's <em>two pumps</em> taped together.","hookSub":"One loop reloads oxygen. One loop delivers it. Stop memorizing chambers and just follow a single drop of blood around the circle.","formula":"<span class=\"warm\">RIGHT side</span> takes in deoxygenated blood &rarr; pumps it to the <span class=\"warm\">lungs</span> to reload<br/><span class=\"teal\">LEFT ventricle</span> takes in oxygenated blood &rarr; pumps it to the <span class=\"teal\">whole body</span><br/><span class=\"gold\">Right = to the lungs. Left = to the world.</span>","formulaSub":"Right pumps blood that <em>needs</em> oxygen. Left pumps blood that <em>has</em> it. That one split tells you every chamber's job.","bulletsHead":"Trace one drop","bullets":["Used-up blood from the body dumps into the <em>right atrium</em> &rarr; drops to the <em>right ventricle</em>.","Right ventricle pushes it out the <em>pulmonary artery</em> to the lungs to grab oxygen.","Oxygen-loaded blood returns from the lungs to the <em>left atrium</em> &rarr; drops to the <em>left ventricle</em>.","Left ventricle blasts it out the <em>aorta</em> to every cell in the body. Loop repeats."],"caption":"Two pumps, two loops: right to the lungs, left to the world. Follow the drop and the heart draws itself.","trapH":"\"Artery\" does NOT mean oxygen-rich","trapSub":"The pulmonary artery carries <em>deoxygenated</em> blood. \"Artery\" only tells you blood is leaving the heart &mdash; that's a direction, not an oxygen level. So don't auto-label every artery as oxygen-rich; the pulmonary artery is the classic exception the DAT loves to test.","lockWord":"Right repairs, Left delivers.","lockDecode":"<span class=\"warm\">Right</span> ships blood to the lungs to be <em>repaired</em> (re-oxygenated); <span class=\"teal\">Left</span> ventricle <em>delivers</em> that oxygen to every cell. Which side works harder on the long trip? The <span class=\"gold\">left ventricle</span> &mdash; that's why its wall is the thickest.","speak":{"hook":"Everybody draws the heart as one pump. It is not. It is two pumps sitting side by side, each running its own loop. Once you see that split, the whole thing gets easy.","formula":"Here is the only rule you need. The right side takes in tired, deoxygenated blood from the body and pumps it to the lungs to reload on oxygen. The left ventricle takes that fresh blood and pumps it out to the whole body. Right goes to the lungs, left goes to the world.","trap":"Here is where people get burned. The pulmonary artery carries deoxygenated blood, even though it is called an artery. Artery just means the blood is leaving the heart -> it says nothing about oxygen. So do not auto-label every artery as oxygen-rich.","lock":"Lock it in like this: right repairs, left delivers. The right side ships blood to the lungs to get repaired, and the left ventricle delivers that oxygen to every cell. Since the left has to reach your entire body, its wall is the thickest -> that is not a coincidence."}},"qr-fractions":{"tag":"QR - one move","title":"Fractions & Decimals: One Number, Then Compare","hookTag":"QR - one sentence","hookWord":"Stop wrestling the fraction.<br/>It's just a <em>number wearing a costume</em>.","hookSub":"Turn the whole word problem into one clean number first. Once it's a single number, comparing is trivial.","formula":"<span class=\"gold\">Words &rarr; one number &rarr; compare.</span><br/><span class=\"teal\">\"of\" = multiply.</span><br/><span class=\"warm\">Multiply before you add.</span>","formulaSub":"\"1/4 of 60, plus 5\" = (1/4 x 60) + 5 = 15 + 5 = 20. The \"of\" fires first, then you add.","bulletsHead":"The 4 steps","bullets":["<em>Read and translate</em> - every \"of\" you see becomes a times sign.","<em>Do the inside first</em> - run the multiply (the fraction-of) before any adding.","<em>Put it in one form</em> - convert fractions to decimals, or to a common denominator, so they line up.","<em>Now compare</em> - it's one number against another, biggest wins."],"caption":"\"of\" means times. Multiply before you add. One number beats a wall of words.","trapH":"Adding before you multiply","trapSub":"Students read \"1/4 of 60 plus 5\" and add 60 + 5 first. Wrong. The \"of\" is a multiply, and it always fires before the plus - it's parentheses in disguise.","lockWord":"\"Of\" is a times sign.","lockDecode":"Anytime you read <span class=\"gold\">\"of\"</span>, write <span class=\"teal\">x</span> &rarr; then finish <em>that</em> piece before you add. Words collapse into one number, and one number is easy to compare.","speak":{"hook":"Here is the reframe. A fraction is not a puzzle to survive, it is just a number wearing a costume. Your whole job is to rip the costume off and turn the words into one clean number. Once it is a single number, comparing is trivial.","formula":"The move is three words: words, one number, compare. When you read the word 'of', write a times sign. One third of ninety means one third times ninety, which is thirty. And you do that inside operation before you add anything.","trap":"The trap that eats points is adding before you multiply. You see 'one fourth of sixty plus five' and your hand wants to do sixty plus five first. Do not. The 'of' is a multiply, and multiply fires before the plus. Treat it like it is already in parentheses.","lock":"Lock it in: 'of' is a times sign. Every time you see the word 'of', you write times, you finish that piece first, then you add. Words become one number, and one number is easy to compare."}},"qr-algebra":{"tag":"QR - one move","title":"Solve for x: both sides, outside in","hookTag":"The reframe","hookWord":"x is <em>gift-wrapped</em>.<br/>Unwrap it in reverse.","hookSub":"Whatever got done to x <em>last</em>, you undo <em>first</em> — and you do it to both sides so the scale stays balanced.","formula":"<span class=\"gold\">Same move, both sides.</span><br/>Then undo the <span class=\"warm\">last</span> operation first: <span class=\"warm\">strip + and &minus;</span> &rarr; <span class=\"teal\">strip &times; and &divide;</span> last.","formulaSub":"It's the reverse of PEMDAS. On 3x + 7, x got multiplied then added — so the add came last, and you peel it off first; the multiply came first, so you peel it off last.","bulletsHead":"The move, on 3x + 7 = 22","bullets":["<em>Spot the layers.</em> x was multiplied by 3, then 7 was added on top.","<em>Peel the outside.</em> Subtract 7 from <em>both</em> sides &rarr; 3x = 15.","<em>Peel again.</em> Divide <em>both</em> sides by 3 &rarr; x = 5.","<em>Check.</em> 3(5) + 7 = 22. It holds — you're done."],"caption":"You're not \"moving things over.\" You're doing the same move to both sides and peeling x free, outside layer first.","trapH":"Dividing before you subtract","trapSub":"On 3x + 7 = 22, if you divide first you have to divide <em>every</em> term: x + 7/3 = 22/3. It's legal but ugly — and almost everyone forgets to divide the 7. Subtract first, keep it clean.","lockWord":"Both sides, outside in.","lockDecode":"<span class=\"gold\">Both sides</span> = whatever you do, do to each side so the scale stays balanced. <span class=\"teal\">Outside in</span> = undo the <em>last</em> thing done to x first — peel the <span class=\"warm\">add/subtract</span> layer, then the <span class=\"teal\">multiply/divide</span> layer.","speak":{"hook":"Here's the reframe that makes algebra click. x isn't hiding -- it's gift-wrapped in layers of operations. Your whole job is to unwrap it one layer at a time, doing the exact same move to both sides so the equation stays balanced.","formula":"The trick is the order you unwrap. Whatever happened to x last, you undo first. So you strip off the plus and minus before you touch the times and divide -- that's the reverse of PEMDAS. Same move on both sides, every time.","trap":"Here's where people blow it. On 3x plus 7 equals 22, they divide by 3 first. That's legal, but now you have to divide the 7 too -- you get x plus seven-thirds equals twenty-two-thirds, and almost everybody forgets that 7. Subtract first, keep it clean.","lock":"Lock it in: both sides, outside in. Both sides keeps the scale balanced. Outside in means undo the last thing done to x first -- peel the add and subtract layer off, then the multiply and divide layer. Do that and x falls right out."}},"qr-ratios":{"tag":"QR - one move","title":"Ratios &amp; Proportions: Same Recipe, Bigger Batch","hookTag":"QR - Ratios","hookWord":"Two matching ratios are the <em>same recipe</em> &mdash;<br/>one just makes a <em>bigger batch</em>.","hookSub":"Stop staring at the numbers. A proportion is one mix at two sizes. See that, and the unknown falls out in a single line.","formula":"<span class=\"gold\">3/4 = x/12</span><br/>Cross-multiply &rarr; 3 &times; 12 = 4 &times; x<br/>Or scale: bottom went <span class=\"teal\">&times;3</span> (4&rarr;12), so top does too &rarr; x = <span class=\"teal\">9</span>","formulaSub":"Cross-multiply and scaling are the exact same trick. Pick whichever is faster for the numbers in front of you.","bulletsHead":"The move in 3 steps","bullets":["Write both ratios as fractions and <em>set them equal</em>. Keep units in the same spot &mdash; miles-over-hours equals miles-over-hours, never flipped.","Cross-multiply: <em>top-left times bottom-right</em> equals <em>bottom-left times top-right</em>. That clears the fractions in one stroke.","Solve the one-step equation for the unknown, then gut-check: what factor scaled one side? The other side rides the <em>same factor</em>."],"caption":"Same recipe, bigger batch. Every ratio problem on the DAT is just that &mdash; set them equal, cross-multiply, done.","trapH":"Adding when you should multiply","trapSub":"Ratios scale by <em>multiplying</em>, not adding. 2:3 becomes 4:6 &mdash; never 4:5. Bump one part by a number instead of a factor and you have quietly changed the mix.","lockWord":"Same recipe, bigger batch.","lockDecode":"Equal ratios are one recipe cooked at two sizes. Whatever you <span class=\"teal\">multiply one part by</span>, multiply the other by the <span class=\"gold\">same factor</span> &mdash; cross-multiply is just that rule doing the algebra for you.","speak":{"hook":"Here is the reframe that makes ratios click. A ratio is a recipe, and two equal ratios are the same recipe at two batch sizes. Nothing about the mix changes -> you just made more of it.","formula":"Write both ratios as fractions and set them equal, lining up the units so the same thing sits on top of each side. Then cross-multiply: top-left times bottom-right equals bottom-left times top-right, and solve. Faster shortcut when the numbers are clean: see what one side got multiplied by, then hit the other side with the same factor.","trap":"Here is the trap that eats points. Ratios scale by multiplying, not adding. Two to three does not become four to five, it becomes four to six -> same factor on both parts, every single time.","lock":"Lock it in: same recipe, bigger batch. Whatever you multiply one part by, multiply the other by the same factor. Cross-multiply is just that rule doing the algebra for you."}},"qr-percentages":{"tag":"QR - one move","title":"Percentages: two moves, every question","hookTag":"QR - Percentages","hookWord":"A price goes up 25%, then down 20%.<br/>You're <em>exactly</em> back where you started.","hookSub":"Not down 5 percent. Percent change <em>multiplies</em> &mdash; and that one fact runs the whole topic.","formula":"<span class=\"gold\">is / of = percent / 100</span><br/>Every change is a multiplier: <span class=\"teal\">up 25% &rarr; &times;1.25</span>, <span class=\"warm\">down 20% &rarr; &times;0.80</span>","formulaSub":"One line translates any \"what percent of\" question. Then you multiply the changes in a row &mdash; never add them.","bulletsHead":"The moves","bullets":["<em>Translate:</em> \"is\" is the top, \"of\" is the bottom. \"What is 30% of 80?\" &rarr; is/80 = 30/100 &rarr; is = 24.","<em>Change up:</em> add to 100, then divide. Up 25% means &times;(125/100) = <span class=\"teal\">&times;1.25</span>.","<em>Change down:</em> subtract from 100. Down 20% means &times;(80/100) = <span class=\"warm\">&times;0.80</span>.","<em>Stack them:</em> multiply the factors, never add the percents. 1.25 &times; 0.80 = <span class=\"gold\">1.00</span> &mdash; dead even."],"caption":"Two moves run all of percentages: is/of = percent/100, and every change is a multiplier. Up 25% then down 20% = exactly even. Multiply, never add.","trapH":"Adding the percent changes","trapSub":"Up 25% then down 20% is NOT down 5% net. You multiply &times;1.25 &times;0.80 = 1.00. After the first step the base moved, so the second percent is taken from a bigger number &mdash; you can't just add.","lockWord":"Is over of. Change multiplies.","lockDecode":"<span class=\"gold\">is/of = percent/100</span> translates every percent question; every <span class=\"teal\">change becomes a multiplier</span> (add or subtract from 100, then divide), and you <span class=\"warm\">multiply them in a row &mdash; never add</span>.","speak":{"hook":"Here is the one that trips everyone up. A price goes up twenty-five percent, then down twenty percent. Most people say you are down five. You are actually exactly back where you started.","formula":"Two moves cover all of percentages. First, is over of equals percent over one hundred -- that translates any word problem. Second, a percent change is a multiplier: up twenty-five percent is times one point two five, down twenty percent is times zero point eight.","trap":"The number one trap is adding percent changes together. Do not. Up twenty-five then down twenty is not minus five. You multiply one point two five times zero point eight, which equals exactly one. The base changed after the first step, so you cannot just add the percents.","lock":"Lock it in: is over of, change multiplies. Translate with is over of equals percent over one hundred, turn every change into a multiplier, and multiply them in a row -- never add."}},"qr-exponents":{"tag":"QR - one move","title":"Exponents & Roots: Keep the Base, Move the Count","hookTag":"QR - one move","hookWord":"Stop multiplying.<br/>Start <em>counting</em>.","hookSub":"2^3 just means three 2's multiplied together. Once you read the exponent as a <em>count</em> of the base, every rule turns into add or subtract.","formula":"Same base, one rule:<br/><span class=\"teal\">multiply &rarr; add the exponents</span><br/><span class=\"warm\">divide &rarr; subtract the exponents</span><br/><span class=\"gold\">root = a fraction on top; negative = flip it under 1</span>","formulaSub":"Example: 2^3 times 2^4 = 2^7, and 9^(1/2) = 3, while 2^(-3) = 1/8.","bulletsHead":"The 4 moves","bullets":["Same base, <em>multiplying</em>? Add: 2^3 times 2^4 = 2^7 (that's 3 + 4).","Same base, <em>dividing</em>? Subtract: 2^7 over 2^4 = 2^3 (that's 7 minus 4).","A <em>root</em> is a fractional power: sqrt(x) = x^(1/2), and cube-root of x = x^(1/3).","A <em>negative</em> exponent flips it: 2^(-3) = 1/2^3 = 1/8."],"caption":"Same base? Stop multiplying and start counting: add to combine, subtract to cancel, flip for a negative, fraction for a root. One rule runs the whole chapter.","trapH":"You changed the base.","trapSub":"2^3 times 2^4 is 2^7, <em>not</em> 4^7 — the base stays put, only the exponent grows. And multiplying means you <em>add</em> the exponents, not multiply them.","lockWord":"Keep the base, move the count.","lockDecode":"The base never changes. Every operation just moves the exponent: <span class=\"teal\">multiply adds to it</span>, <span class=\"warm\">divide subtracts from it</span>, <span class=\"gold\">a root splits it into a fraction, and a negative sends it under 1</span>.","speak":{"hook":"Stop reading an exponent as a scary multiplication problem. Two to the third just means three twos multiplied together, a count of how many times the base shows up. Once you see that little number as a count, every rule turns into simple adding and subtracting.","formula":"Here is the entire move for the same base. If you are multiplying, add the exponents. If you are dividing, subtract them. A root is just a fraction on top, so the square root is the one-half power, and a negative exponent flips the whole thing under one.","trap":"The number one trap is touching the base. Two to the third times two to the fourth is two to the seventh, not four to the seventh, because the base never changes, only the count on top grows. And remember, multiplying means you add the exponents, not multiply them.","lock":"Keep the base, move the count. Multiply adds to the count, divide takes from it, a root splits it into a fraction, and a negative sends it downstairs under one. Say that one line and the whole chapter collapses."}},"qr-geometry":{"tag":"QR - one move","title":"QR Geometry: draw it, then plug it","hookTag":"QR - one move","hookWord":"Draw it<br/>before you<br/><em>solve</em> it","hookSub":"You are not memorizing twenty formulas. A labeled picture turns a word problem into plug-and-chug.","formula":"<span class=\"gold\">Draw &rarr; Label &rarr; Plug</span><br/><span class=\"teal\">Flat = area = square units</span><br/><span class=\"warm\">Solid = volume = cubic units</span>","formulaSub":"Same numbers, different formula &mdash; the units tell you flat or solid.","bulletsHead":"The 4 moves","bullets":["<em>Draw and label.</em> Sketch the shape and write every number on it: radius, side, height. Handed a diameter? Halve it to get <em>r</em> first.","<em>Ask flat or solid.</em> Covering a surface is area (square units); filling a space is volume (cubic units).","<em>Plug the match.</em> Circle area = pi x r squared. Box volume = l x w x h. Cylinder = pi x r squared x h.","<em>Check your units.</em> Answer came out in square units but they wanted volume? You grabbed the area formula. Swap it."],"caption":"Stop memorizing twenty geometry formulas. Draw it, label it, then ask one question: flat or solid? Square units = area, cubic units = volume. #DAT #DATprep #QuantitativeReasoning #predental #dentalschool","trapH":"Right numbers, wrong dimension","trapSub":"Computing area when the question asks how much fills the shape. If the answer needs cubic units, you need a volume formula. And always halve a diameter before it becomes r.","lockWord":"Squared is flat, cubed is full.","lockDecode":"<span class=\"teal\">Square units</span> = a 2-D flat area; <span class=\"warm\">cubic units</span> = a 3-D solid volume. Draw it, label it, and let the units the answer needs point you to the formula.","speak":{"hook":"Here is the thing about DAT geometry: you are not memorizing twenty formulas, you are answering one question, flat or solid. So before you touch a single number, draw the shape and label it. A picture with the numbers written on it turns a scary word problem into plug and chug.","formula":"Three moves. Draw it, label every number right on the drawing, then plug the matching formula. A flat shape gives you area in square units, a solid shape gives you volume in cubic units. The units literally tell you which formula you need.","trap":"The number one trap is using the right numbers in the wrong dimension. If the question asks how much fills the shape, that is volume, cubic units, so an area formula will burn you. And if they hand you a diameter, cut it in half to get the radius before you plug anything in.","lock":"Lock it in: squared is flat, cubed is full. Square units mean two-D area, cubic units mean three-D volume. Read the units the answer needs, and the exponent points you straight to the right formula."}},"qr-trig":{"tag":"QR - one move","title":"Trig Is Just Two Triangles","hookTag":"QR - Trigonometry","hookWord":"You're not <em>calculating</em> trig.<br/>You're <em>recognizing</em> two triangles.","hookSub":"The DAT almost never makes you compute a sine from scratch, and its on-screen calculator can't even do trig functions. Every trig question hides one of two special triangles. Learn them cold and you just read off the answer.","formula":"<span class=\"gold\">SOH &nbsp; CAH &nbsp; TOA</span><br/>sin = <span class=\"teal\">opp</span> / hyp<br/>cos = <span class=\"teal\">adj</span> / hyp<br/>tan = <span class=\"teal\">opp</span> / <span class=\"teal\">adj</span>","formulaSub":"The three letters <em>are</em> the fractions: S-O-H means Sine = Opposite over Hypotenuse. Read them left to right and you physically can't swap them.","bulletsHead":"The 4 steps","bullets":["<strong>Label from your angle:</strong> the side touching it is <em>adjacent</em>, the side across from it is <em>opposite</em>, the long slanted side is the <em>hypotenuse</em>.","<strong>Pick the ratio the question gives you</strong> &mdash; sin, cos, or tan &mdash; and write its two sides straight from SOH-CAH-TOA.","Memorize <strong>30-60-90 &rarr; 1, root3, 2</strong> (short leg, long leg, hypotenuse).","Memorize <strong>45-45-90 &rarr; 1, 1, root2</strong> (two equal legs, then the hypotenuse)."],"caption":"Master two triangles and SOH-CAH-TOA, and DAT trig stops being math &mdash; it becomes a lookup.","trapH":"Opposite and adjacent flip with the angle","trapSub":"Adjacent and opposite are <em>not</em> fixed sides &mdash; they swap depending on which angle you're standing at. Only the hypotenuse, across from the 90, never moves. Always relabel from the angle in the question <em>before</em> you pick a ratio.","lockWord":"Read the letters, chant the triangles.","lockDecode":"The letters hand you the ratio &mdash; <span class=\"gold\">S</span>ine is <span class=\"gold\">O</span>pp over <span class=\"gold\">H</span>yp. The chant hands you the numbers &mdash; <span class=\"gold\">30-60-90 is 1, root3, 2</span> and <span class=\"gold\">45-45-90 is 1, 1, root2</span>. Two things memorized, the whole section handled.","speak":{"hook":"Here's the reframe. The DAT almost never makes you actually compute a sine or cosine from scratch, and the on-screen calculator can't even do trig. Every trig question is hiding one of two special triangles you can memorize in an afternoon. So you're not calculating, you're recognizing.","formula":"SOH-CAH-TOA. The letters are the fractions. S-O-H is sine equals opposite over hypotenuse. C-A-H is cosine equals adjacent over hypotenuse. T-O-A is tangent equals opposite over adjacent. Read them left to right and you physically cannot mix them up.","trap":"Here's the one that gets people. Opposite and adjacent are not fixed sides. They flip depending on which angle you are looking from. The only side that never moves is the hypotenuse, the long one across from the right angle. So before you pick a ratio, relabel the triangle from the angle in the question.","lock":"Lock it in like this. Read the letters, chant the triangles. The letters hand you the ratio. The chant, one, root three, two, and one, one, root two, hands you the numbers. Memorize those two things and DAT trig turns into a lookup."}},"qr-probability":{"tag":"QR - one move","title":"Probability, decoded in two keywords","hookTag":"QR - Probability","hookWord":"Probability is just<br/><em>wins over total</em>","hookSub":"Every question is favorable outcomes over all outcomes. After that, two keywords run the whole thing.","formula":"<span class=\"gold\">P = wins / total</span><br/><span class=\"warm\">AND (independent) &rarr; multiply</span><br/><span class=\"teal\">OR (can't overlap) &rarr; add</span><br/><span class=\"gold\">at least one &rarr; 1 &minus; none</span>","formulaSub":"<span class=\"warm\">AND</span> shrinks it (fractions multiply down). <span class=\"teal\">OR</span> grows it (pieces add up). <span class=\"gold\">At least one</span> flips to the easy side.","bulletsHead":"The 4 reads","bullets":["Base every problem the same way: <em>favorable outcomes over total outcomes</em>. That one fraction <em>is</em> a probability.","See <em>AND</em> or \"both\" with independent events? <em>Multiply</em>. Two heads in a row = 1/2 x 1/2 = <em>1/4</em>.","See <em>OR</em> with outcomes that can't both happen? <em>Add</em>. Roll a 2 or a 5 = 1/6 + 1/6 = <em>1/3</em>.","See <em>at least one</em>? Don't list cases. Do <em>1 minus the chance none happen</em>. At least one head in 3 flips = 1 &minus; (1/2)&sup3; = <em>7/8</em>."],"caption":"One fraction and two keywords run the entire probability section. Wins over total to start; AND-multiply, OR-add, at-least-flip to finish.","trapH":"Grinding \"at least one\" the long way","trapSub":"The second you start adding up every winning case, you'll miss a combination and burn a minute. <em>At least one</em> is your signal to flip it: <span class=\"gold\">1 minus the chance it never happens</span>. One subtraction, done.","lockWord":"Multiply the ANDs, add the ORs, flip the at-least-ones.","lockDecode":"<span class=\"warm\">AND = multiply</span> (both must hit), <span class=\"teal\">OR = add</span> (either one counts), and <span class=\"gold\">at least one = 1 &minus; none</span>. Two keywords and a flip cover every probability question the DAT throws.","speak":{"hook":"Probability looks scary, but it is really just counting. Every single question is your wins divided by the total number of outcomes. Get that fraction, and you are most of the way there.","formula":"From there, two keywords do the work. AND with independent events means multiply, like one half x one half for two heads in a row. OR with outcomes that cannot overlap means add, like one sixth plus one sixth for rolling a 2 or a 5. And at least one means take 1 and subtract the chance that none of them happen.","trap":"Here is where people lose the minute. They see at least one and start listing every winning case, then miss a combination. Do not. At least one is your signal to flip it: 1 minus the chance it never happens. At least one head in three flips is just 1 minus one half cubed, which is seven eighths.","lock":"So lock it in: multiply the ANDs, add the ORs, and flip the at-least-ones. Wins over total to start, two keywords to finish. That is the whole probability section."}},"qr-statistics":{"tag":"QR - one move","title":"Mean chases the tail","hookTag":"Stats - one move","hookWord":"The mean has to <em>touch every number</em>.<br/>The median just takes a <em>seat</em>.","hookSub":"Why one monster value can wreck the average, and can't touch the median.","formula":"<span class=\"warm\">Mean</span> = balance point &rarr; one big value <span class=\"warm\">drags it</span> toward the tail<br/><span class=\"teal\">Median</span> = the middle seat &rarr; <span class=\"teal\">ignores how far</span> the outlier sits<br/><span class=\"gold\">Skewed data? The mean runs toward the long tail.</span>","formulaSub":"Right-skew (tail on the right): mean &gt; median. Left-skew: mean &lt; median.","bulletsHead":"Read any set in 4 moves","bullets":["<em>Spot the tail.</em> An outlier or a long stretch on one side is your alarm bell.","<em>Median holds.</em> Line the numbers up and take the middle value (average the two middles if the count is even). It won't budge for one extreme.","<em>Mean slides.</em> It drifts toward the tail. Long right tail &rarr; mean climbs above the median.","<em>SD swells too.</em> Its distance from the mean gets squared, so one far value stretches the spread more than you'd expect."],"caption":"The median doesn't care how rich the billionaire in the room is. The mean can't stop staring.","trapH":"Grabbing the mean on a lopsided set","trapSub":"If there's an outlier or a long tail and the question wants the <em>typical</em> value, the mean already got yanked toward the extreme. That's the median's job.","lockWord":"Mean chases the tail.","lockDecode":"<span class=\"warm\">Mean</span> runs toward the long tail; <span class=\"teal\">median</span> holds the middle. Skewed data &rarr; trust the median for the typical value.","speak":{"hook":"Here's the reframe. The mean has to touch every single number, so it's really a balance point. Drop one giant value into the set and the whole thing tips toward it. The median does not care how big that value is. It just lines everyone up and takes the middle seat.","formula":"So when the data is skewed, the mean chases the tail. Long tail on the right, the mean climbs above the median. Long tail on the left, it slides below. The median barely moves either way, and that is exactly why we call it resistant. One more thing: standard deviation swells too, because that far value's distance from the mean gets squared before you average it.","trap":"The trap is grabbing the mean when the set is lopsided. If there is an outlier or a long tail and the question asks for the typical value, the mean already got yanked toward the extreme. Reach for the median instead.","lock":"Lock it in: mean chases the tail. The mean runs toward the extreme, the median holds the middle. Skewed data, trust the median."}},"qr-word-problems":{"tag":"QR - one move","title":"Rate, Work &amp; Mixture: One Engine","hookTag":"The reframe","hookWord":"The math is easy.<br/>The <em>setup</em> is the test.","hookSub":"Miles, jobs, salt in water -- it's all one sentence in disguise.","formula":"<span class=\"gold\">rate &times; time = amount</span> &mdash; the engine<br/><span class=\"teal\">work together &rarr; add the rates</span><br/>mixture &rarr; <span class=\"teal\">the stuff</span> &divide; <span class=\"warm\">the total</span>","formulaSub":"One engine. Three costumes.","bulletsHead":"The 3-move setup","bullets":["<em>Name the rate.</em> '60 mph' means 60 miles every 1 hour -- the units tell you what times what makes what.","<em>Work problem? Flip to rates.</em> One job in 4 hrs = 1/4 job per hour. Add the rates, then flip back to get the time together.","<em>Mixture? Part over whole.</em> Concentration = amount of the one thing &divide; total amount -- track the stuff and the total separately.","<em>Fill the blank.</em> Drop your numbers into rate &times; time = amount and isolate whatever's missing."],"caption":"DAT word problems aren't hiding hard math -- they're hiding the setup. rate x time = amount. Rates add, times don't. Mixture is part over whole. Nail the sentence and every one folds.","trapH":"Adding the times, not the rates","trapSub":"\"4 hrs plus 6 hrs = 10\" feels right and it's dead wrong -- two people finish <em>faster</em> than either alone. Add the rates: 1/4 + 1/6 = 5/12 per hour &rarr; flip &rarr; 12/5 = 2.4 hrs. Sanity check: the answer is always less than your fastest worker.","lockWord":"One engine, three costumes","lockDecode":"<span class=\"gold\">rate &times; time = amount</span> is the only formula. Distance wears it as miles per hour, work wears it as jobs per hour, mixture wears it as <span class=\"teal\">stuff</span> over <span class=\"warm\">total</span>. Name the rate and the costume falls off.","speak":{"hook":"Here's what nobody tells you about DAT word problems. The math is almost never the hard part -- the setup is. The second you can name the rate, the arithmetic is easy.","formula":"There's really just one engine: rate times time equals amount. When two things work together, you add the rates, never the times. And a mixture is a rate too -- the amount of your stuff divided by the total.","trap":"Here's the classic miss. One person paints a room in 4 hours, another in 6, so together it's 10, right? No -- two people are faster than one, so the answer has to be under 4. Turn them into rates: one fourth plus one sixth is five twelfths of a room per hour. Flip that and you get twelve fifths, which is 2.4 hours.","lock":"So lock this in: one engine, three costumes. Rate times time equals amount is all of it -- miles per hour, jobs per hour, or stuff over total. Name the rate, and the costume falls right off."}}});
